      "math_academy_id": "q-31288",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Find ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ if $f(x)=-6\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we just take the constant out of the derivative and use the result for the derivative of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-6\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =-6(-{csc}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =6{csc}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =6{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =6{(2)}^{2} \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
